iOS/ARM64EC: a lean path for L1 misses, a bigger L1 for hot threads

With the L2 off (DisableL2Cache) every miss of the JIT's L1 probe calls
CompileBlock, and on iOS that path ran every diagnostic in front of the
lookup before it found the block. Cyberpunk 2077 (patch 2.5, iPhone19,2,
2026-11-06) missed 250-460 K times a second during play, 99% of them for
blocks compiled long before ([CB_SUMMARY] real_compiles flat, [l1-miss]
200-440 K/s), with its job threads' L1 at the 128 K-entry iOS cap.

- CompileBlock: the lookup first. Cross-process work, the invalidation
  lock shared, FindBlock, as the full path does them; a found block returns
  at once. No block, a call-return pointer outside its stack or emission
  locks held by this thread take the full path as before.
  MADEIRA_FEX_FAST_MISS=0 restores the old order.
- A thread still missing MADEIRA_FEX_BIG_L1_RATE (20000) times a second at
  the cap gets an L1 of MADEIRA_FEX_BIG_L1 K entries (512 K, 8 MB) of its
  own, for at most MADEIRA_FEX_BIG_L1_THREADS (6) threads: 48 MB at most.
  Allocated by the owning thread with no lock held, switched in under the
  lookup read lock. MADEIRA_FEX_BIG_L1=0 turns it off. [l1-big] lines.
- UpdateDynamicL1Stats runs its work every 256th miss of a thread: the
  process-wide [l1-miss] count adds 256 at a time instead of an atomic on
  every miss, and the period uses cntvct_el0 instead of
  std::chrono::system_clock, whose GetProcAddress'd kernelbase call is an
  exec fault and a Mach exception on iOS (~1,000 a second, [xlate-exec]).

ARM64EC module only (FEX_IOS_HOST and ARCHITECTURE_arm64ec); the WOW64
module and the native iOS FEXCore build are unchanged. Applies on
willfaust/FEX 1dc6ed4 after 0001. Madeira-only: not for FEX-Emu.

